{"id":"storm-shadow","slug":"storm-shadow","name":"Storm Shadow/SCALP EG","aliases":["SPEAR4","SPEAR 4","SCALP EG","SCALP-EG","SCALP missile","SCALP (missile)","MBDA Storm Shadow","Stormshadow missile","Storm Shadow missile","Storm Shadow (missile)","Storm Shadow"],"kind":"missile","category":"cruise-missiles","subcategory":"Franco-British cruise missile","description":"The Storm Shadow is a Franco-British low-observable, long-range air-launched cruise missile developed since 1994 by Matra and British Aerospace, and now manufactured by MBDA. \"Storm Shadow\" is the weapon's British name; in France it is called SCALP-EG. The missile is based on the French-developed Apache anti-runway cruise missile, but differs in that it carries a unitary warhead instead of cluster munitions.","history":"Matra and British Aerospace were selected as the prime contractors for the Conventionally Armed Standoff Missile (CASOM) in July 1996; their Storm Shadow missile beat submissions from McDonnell Douglas, Texas Instruments/Short Brothers, Hughes/Smiths Industries, Daimler-Benz Aerospace/Bofors, GEC-Marconi and Rafael. The Storm Shadow design was based on Matra's Apache anti-runway cruise missile. A development and production contract was signed in February 1997, by which time Matra and BAe had completed the merger of their missile businesses to form Matra BAe Dynamics. France ordered 500 SCALP missiles in January 1998.\nThe first successful fully guided firing of the Storm Shadow/SCALP EG took place at the CEL Biscarosse range in France at the end of December 2000 from a Mirage 2000N.\nThe first flight of Storm Shadow missiles on the Eurofighter Typhoon took place on 27 November 2013 at Decimomannu Air Base in Italy, and was performed by Alenia Aermacchi using instrumented production aircraft 2.\nThe SCALP EG and Storm Shadow are identical except for how they integrate with the aircraft.\nIn July 2016, the UK's MoD awarded a £28 million contract to support the Storm Shadow over the next five years.","facts":[],"specifications":[{"group":"Propulsion & Guidance","items":[{"label":"Engine","value":"Microturbo TRI 60-30 turbojet"},{"label":"Guidance","value":"GPS, INS, IIR & TERPROM"},{"label":"Steering","value":"6 tailplanes (4 vertical & 2 horizontal)"}]},{"group":"Dimensions","items":[{"label":"Length","value":"5.1 m","numeric":5.1,"unit":"m"},{"label":"Wingspan","value":"3 m","numeric":3,"unit":"m"}]},{"group":"Performance","items":[{"label":"Operational Range","value":"550 km","numeric":550,"unit":"km"},{"label":"Speed","value":"0.95 Mach","numeric":0.95,"unit":"Mach"}]},{"group":"Production","items":[{"label":"Designer","value":"Matra BAe Dynamics"},{"label":"Designed","value":"1994–2001"},{"label":"Manufacturer","value":"MBDA"},{"label":"Unit Cost","value":"2.0M (FY2023) (2.5M)","numeric":2,"unit":"M"}]},{"group":"Service","items":[{"label":"Origin","value":"France"},{"label":"Origin","value":"United Kingdom"},{"label":"In Service","value":"2003–present"}]}],"specIndex":{"Engine":"Microturbo TRI 60-30 turbojet","Guidance":"GPS, INS, IIR & TERPROM","Steering":"6 tailplanes (4 vertical & 2 horizontal)","Length":"5.1 m","Wingspan":"3 m","Operational Range":"550 km","Speed":"0.95 Mach","Designer":"Matra BAe Dynamics","Designed":"1994–2001","Manufacturer":"MBDA","Unit Cost":"2.0M (FY2023) (2.5M)","Origin":"France","In Service":"2003–present"},"images":{"hero":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3f/RAF_Museum%2C_Colindale%2C_London_-_DSC06025.JPG","descriptionUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RAF_Museum,_Colindale,_London_-_DSC06025.JPG","width":3240,"height":2160,"license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","attribution":"Rept0n1x"},"thumb":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/RAF_Museum%2C_Colindale%2C_London_-_DSC06025.JPG/330px-RAF_Museum%2C_Colindale%2C_London_-_DSC06025.JPG"},"gallery":[{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3f/R